"""Batch B06: the domains in diagram (1.3) (R014, R015).

Independent reading (Section 8 after its review).

1. The limit (Section 8.2, before Lemma lat:zeta): for h = P/Q in R[T]_dag, deg h = deg P - deg Q;
   lim_{T -> infty} h is defined when deg h <= 0, and the elements of degree <= 0 form a subgroup
   on which the limit is additive. The polynomial T has degree 1 and no limit, so -lim is not
   defined on all of R[T]_dag, as the diagram shows. The proof of Theorem direct:mil gives, for
   [U] = [(U, f, l)] with l in Z, that Z([U]) lies in R[T]_dag, has degree <= 0, and
   lim Z([U]) = -(Theta o E^diamond)([U]). No Hadamard compatibility of the limit is used.
2. The domain of Z: the superscript natural means (Remark dia:to:db:dag) the intersection of the
   rings K^natural_m on which eta o h_m is defined; rationality is proved only for classes with a
   constant integral volume form (and their sums). Products of such classes are again of this
   form, so these classes generate a subring, whose elements are sums. By Remark dia:to:db:dag,
   the integral of a class (A, l) with l constant is represented by some (U, f, l), so the integral
   maps the corresponding subring of K muVF^diamond[*] into it. Yimu's blue note says exactly this.
3. Commutativity of the lower part: -lim Z(x) = Theta E^diamond(x) on that subring (proof of
   Theorem direct:mil), and E^diamond = E_b o (forgetting volume forms) by (edb:eb:com).

What the batch changes: Remark dia:to:db:dag names the two subrings K^Z; the diagram uses K^Z in
the right column and the subgroup of degree <= 0 for the domain of -lim; the paragraph under the
diagram states these domains, identifies bold H_m with eta o h_m, and says why the diagram
commutes; Yimu's blue note, now incorporated, is deleted. The notation K^Z is a proposal.
"""

TITLE = 'Section 1 review B06: the zeta diagram'
ANCHORS = ['Then we have a commutative diagram', 'Domain of the limit arrow', 'Incorporate the blue restriction',
           'whose volume form is a constant integer', 'Since a product of two such classes']
SELECT = True

OLD_DIAGRAM = r'''  \square(0,800)/<-`->`->`<-/<1500,400>[{\ggk \VF_*}`{\ggk^\natural \mgVF^\diamond[*]}`{\ggk \RV[*] / (\bm P - 1)}`{\ggk^\natural \mgRV^{\db}[*] / J_{\mathrm{var}}}; `\int`\int^{\diamond}`]
  \square(0,400)/`->`->`/<1500,400>[{\ggk \RV[*] / (\bm P - 1)}`{\ggk^\natural \mgRV^{\db}[*] / J_{\mathrm{var}}}`\gmv`\gmv{[[\A]^{-1}][T]_{\dag}}; `\Theta \circ \bb E_b`Z`]
  \square(0,0)|aamb|/`->`->`=/<1500,400>[\gmv`\gmv{[[\A]^{-1}][T]_{\dag}}`\gmv{[[\A]^{-1}]}`\gmv{[[\A]^{-1}]};'''

NEW_DIAGRAM = (OLD_DIAGRAM.replace(r'\ggk^\natural', r'\ggk^{\Z}')
               .replace(r'\gmv{[[\A]^{-1}][T]_{\dag}}', r'\gmv{[[\A]^{-1}][T]_{\dag}^{\leq 0}}'))

NEW_PARAGRAPH = r'''\ReviewOnly{In the diagram above, the superscript natural is replaced by Z, and the domain of the arrow -lim is the subgroup of degree at most 0. Yimu's blue note after the first sentence below is deleted.}The superscript ``$\Z$'' indicates the subrings generated by the classes of the objects whose volume form is a constant integer (Remark~\ref{dia:to:db:dag}); in the middle row it stands for the image of that subring in the quotient by $J_{\mathrm{var}}$. The integral $\int^\diamond$ maps $\ggk^{\Z} \mgVF^\diamond[*]$ into $\ggk^{\Z} \mgRV^{\db}[*] / J_{\mathrm{var}}$. The map $Z$ assigns, to each class $[\bm U] \in \ggk^{\Z} \mgRV^{\db}[*]$, a power series
\[
Z([\bm U])(T) = \sum_{m \in \Z^+} \bm H_m([\bm U]) T^m \in \ggk^{\hat \mu} \var_{\C}[[\A]^{-1}] \dbra T,
\]
that is, the motivic zeta function of $\bm U$, where $\bm H_m = \bm \eta \circ \bm h_m$ is the rectified version of $H_m$ (\S~\ref{piece:res}). Since $\bm H_m$ vanishes on the elements of $J_{\mathrm{var}}$ in its domain (Lemma~\ref{PG:van}), $Z$ is defined on the quotient. Note that $Z$ is a ring homomorphism with respect to the Hadamard product in the target. It turns out that $Z([\bm U])(T)$ is rational: it belongs to $\gmv[[\A]^{-1}][T]_{\dag}$ and is a quotient $P/Q$ with $\deg P \leq \deg Q$ (see the proof of Theorem~\ref{direct:mil}). The superscript ``$\leq 0$'' denotes the subgroup of these elements of degree at most $0$. On it, the formal process $T \limplies \infty$ mentioned above is defined and additive, and lands us in $\gmv[[\A]^{-1}]$ (\S~\ref{sec:milnor}); this is the map $-\lim$. The lower part of the diagram commutes by (the proof of) Theorem~\ref{direct:mil}: for every $x \in \ggk^{\Z} \mgRV^{\db}[*]/J_{\mathrm{var}}$, $-\lim Z(x)$ is the image of $(\Theta \circ \bb E^\diamond)(x)$, and $\bb E^{\diamond}$ is $\bb E_b$ composed with the forgetful map, by (\ref{edb:eb:com}). If we take $[\bm U] / J_{\mathrm{var}} = \int^{\diamond}([\mdl X_f])$, which is possible since $\mdl X_f$ carries the constant volume form $0$, then the end result is the motivic Milnor fiber $\mathscr S_{f}$.'''

EDITS = [
    (OLD_DIAGRAM, OLD_DIAGRAM, NEW_DIAGRAM, False),
    (r"The superscript ``$\natural$'' means that the integral is restricted to the subring of a sort of ``integrable'' sets.",
     r'then the end result is the motivic Milnor fiber $\mathscr S_{f}$.',
     NEW_PARAGRAPH),
    (r'designates a unique element  in $\gmv[[\A]^{-1}]$.',
     r'designates a unique element  in $\gmv[[\A]^{-1}]$.',
     r'''designates a unique element  in $\gmv[[\A]^{-1}]$.
Let $\ggk^{\Z} \mgRV^{\db}[*]$ be the subring of $\ggk \mgRV^{\db}[*]$ generated by the classes $[(U, f, l)]$ with $l \in \Z$, and $\ggk^{\Z} \mgVF^\diamond[*]$ the subring of $\ggk \mgVF^\diamond[*]$ generated by the classes $[(A, l)]$ with $l \in \Z$. Since a product of two such classes is again of this form, their elements are the finite sums of such classes and their negatives. The first ring is contained in $\ggk^\natural \mgRV^{\db}[*]$, and, by the above, $\int^\diamond$ maps the second into the image of the first in $\ggk \mgRV^{\db}[*]/J_{\mathrm{var}}$, which we write as $\ggk^{\Z} \mgRV^{\db}[*]/J_{\mathrm{var}}$.''',
     True, r'designates a unique element  in $\gmv[[\A]^{-1}]$.'),
]

RESOLUTIONS = {
    '014': r'The arrow $-\lim$ now starts from the subgroup $\gmv[[\A]^{-1}][T]_{\dag}^{\leq 0}$ of the elements of degree at most $0$, on which the limit is defined and additive (Section~\ref{sec:milnor}); by the proof of Theorem~\ref{direct:mil}, the zeta functions of the classes in the diagram lie in it. Compatibility of the limit with the Hadamard product is not used.',
    '015': r'The right column of the diagram is restricted to the subrings $\ggk^{\Z}$ generated by the classes with a constant integral volume form, now defined in Remark~\ref{dia:to:db:dag}; this is the generality in which the proof of Theorem~\ref{direct:mil} establishes rationality and the limit. The rationality claim is stated for these classes, the commutativity of the diagram is justified, and Yimu\'s blue note, now incorporated, is deleted. The general bounded-category claim at the end of Section~\ref{sec:spec:pui} was removed in the Section~8 review.'.replace("\\'", "'"),
}
